WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.036 --> 00:11.606 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Powell's
Press Conference June 13,

00:11.606 --> 00:12.506 align:middle
2018 CHAIRMAN POWELL.

00:12.506 --> 00:12.946 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:12.946 --> 00:13.646 align:middle
Thanks very much for being here.

00:13.646 --> 00:17.546 align:middle
I know that a number of you will
want to talk about the details

00:17.546 --> 00:19.886 align:middle
of our announcement
today, and I am happy to do

00:19.886 --> 00:20.716 align:middle
that in a few minutes.

00:21.846 --> 00:24.376 align:middle
But because monetary
policy affects everyone,

00:24.756 --> 00:26.836 align:middle
I want to start with a
plain-English summary

00:26.836 --> 00:29.816 align:middle
of how the economy is doing,
what my colleagues and I

00:29.816 --> 00:32.016 align:middle
at the Federal Reserve
are trying to do, and why.

00:32.706 --> 00:36.116 align:middle
The main takeaway is that the
economy is doing very well.

00:37.486 --> 00:40.456 align:middle
Most people who want to
find jobs are finding them,

00:40.456 --> 00:42.366 align:middle
and unemployment and
inflation are low.

00:43.656 --> 00:44.896 align:middle
Interest rates have been low

00:44.896 --> 00:47.246 align:middle
for some years while the
economy has been recovering

00:47.246 --> 00:48.466 align:middle
from the financial crisis.

00:49.786 --> 00:54.076 align:middle
For the past few years, we have
been gradually raising interest

00:54.076 --> 00:55.586 align:middle
rates, and along
the way we've tried

00:55.586 --> 00:57.706 align:middle
to explain the reasoning
behind our decisions.

00:58.346 --> 00:59.446 align:middle
In particular, we think

00:59.446 --> 01:01.316 align:middle
that gradually returning
interest rates

01:01.316 --> 01:02.516 align:middle
to a more normal level

01:02.516 --> 01:05.806 align:middle
as the economy strengthens is
the best way the Fed can help

01:05.806 --> 01:08.826 align:middle
sustain an environment in
which American households

01:08.826 --> 01:09.996 align:middle
and businesses can thrive.

01:11.816 --> 01:14.316 align:middle
Today, we've taken another
step in that process

01:14.316 --> 01:17.686 align:middle
by raising our target range for
the federal funds rate by 1/4

01:17.686 --> 01:18.566 align:middle
of a percentage point.

01:20.206 --> 01:22.506 align:middle
My colleagues and I
meet eight times a year

01:23.056 --> 01:26.556 align:middle
and take a fresh look each
time at what is happening

01:26.556 --> 01:28.876 align:middle
in the economy and
consider whether our policy

01:28.876 --> 01:29.786 align:middle
needs adjusting.

01:30.656 --> 01:34.156 align:middle
We don't put our interest
rate decisions on hold

01:34.506 --> 01:37.046 align:middle
or on autopilot, because the
economy can always evolve

01:37.046 --> 01:38.256 align:middle
in unexpected ways.

01:39.396 --> 01:42.316 align:middle
History has shown that moving
interest rates either too

01:42.316 --> 01:45.686 align:middle
quickly or too slowly can
lead to bad economic outcomes.

01:46.686 --> 01:50.016 align:middle
We think the outcomes are likely
to be better overall if we are

01:50.016 --> 01:52.836 align:middle
as clear as possible about what
we are likely to do and why.

01:53.526 --> 01:56.976 align:middle
To that end, we try to give
a sense of our expectations

01:56.976 --> 01:58.456 align:middle
for how the economy will evolve

01:58.866 --> 02:01.036 align:middle
and how our policy
stance may change.

02:02.316 --> 02:05.316 align:middle
As Chairman, I hope to
foster a public conversation

02:05.316 --> 02:07.406 align:middle
about what the Fed is
doing to support a strong

02:07.436 --> 02:08.416 align:middle
and resilient economy.

02:09.286 --> 02:12.476 align:middle
And one practical step in doing
so is to have a press conference

02:12.476 --> 02:15.586 align:middle
like this after every one of
our scheduled FOMC meetings.

02:16.276 --> 02:18.276 align:middle
And we're going to do
that beginning in January.

02:19.516 --> 02:20.896 align:middle
That will give us
more opportunities

02:20.896 --> 02:23.526 align:middle
to explain our actions and
to answer your questions.

02:24.556 --> 02:27.346 align:middle
I want to point out
that having twice

02:27.346 --> 02:30.056 align:middle
as many press conferences
does not signal anything

02:30.106 --> 02:33.436 align:middle
about the timing or pace of
future interest rate changes.

02:34.016 --> 02:36.686 align:middle
This change is only about
improving communications.

02:37.676 --> 02:40.006 align:middle
My FOMC colleagues and
I will also continue

02:40.006 --> 02:41.636 align:middle
to issue our economic
projections

02:41.636 --> 02:43.316 align:middle
on the existing quarterly
schedule.

02:45.006 --> 02:47.636 align:middle
Now, let me go into more
detail over developments

02:47.636 --> 02:49.796 align:middle
in the economy, our
economic projections,

02:49.796 --> 02:50.916 align:middle
and our policy decision.

02:52.566 --> 02:53.996 align:middle
Economic growth appears
to have picked

02:53.996 --> 02:56.846 align:middle
up in the current quarter,
largely reflecting a bounceback

02:56.846 --> 02:57.896 align:middle
in household spending.

02:59.256 --> 03:01.336 align:middle
Business investment
continues to grow strongly,

03:02.206 --> 03:04.636 align:middle
and the overall outlook for
growth remains favorable.

03:05.856 --> 03:08.186 align:middle
Several factors support
this assessment:

03:09.366 --> 03:11.236 align:middle
Fiscal policy is
boosting the economy,

03:11.896 --> 03:14.796 align:middle
ongoing job gains are raising
incomes and confidence,

03:15.696 --> 03:17.516 align:middle
foreign economies
continue to expand,

03:18.416 --> 03:21.126 align:middle
and overall financial
conditions remain accommodative.

03:23.056 --> 03:25.176 align:middle
These observations are
consistent with the projections

03:25.176 --> 03:27.476 align:middle
that Committee participants
submitted for this meeting.

03:28.836 --> 03:30.426 align:middle
The median projection
for the growth

03:30.426 --> 03:35.226 align:middle
of real GDP is 2.8 percent this
year, 2.4 percent next year,

03:35.266 --> 03:37.606 align:middle
and 2 percent in 2020.

03:39.326 --> 03:41.136 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in March,

03:41.536 --> 03:44.146 align:middle
this median growth
path is little changed.

03:44.696 --> 03:49.516 align:middle
In the labor market, job gains
averaged 180,000 per month

03:49.576 --> 03:52.676 align:middle
over the past three months,
well above the pace needed

03:52.676 --> 03:54.416 align:middle
in the longer run
to provide jobs

03:54.416 --> 03:56.606 align:middle
for new entrants
into the workforce.

03:57.136 --> 04:00.396 align:middle
The unemployment rate declined
over the past two months

04:00.396 --> 04:03.926 align:middle
and stood at 3.8 percent
in May, its lowest level

04:03.926 --> 04:05.276 align:middle
in nearly two decades.

04:06.216 --> 04:08.596 align:middle
Meanwhile, the labor force
participation rate has been

04:08.596 --> 04:11.846 align:middle
roughly unchanged
since late 2013.

04:12.446 --> 04:14.516 align:middle
That is a positive sign,
given that the aging

04:14.516 --> 04:16.716 align:middle
of our population is
putting downward pressure

04:16.716 --> 04:17.916 align:middle
on the participation rate.

04:19.176 --> 04:21.196 align:middle
And we expect the job
market to remain strong.

04:22.276 --> 04:24.596 align:middle
As you can see in our Summary
of Economic Projections,

04:24.726 --> 04:26.896 align:middle
the median of Committee
participants' projections

04:26.896 --> 04:29.486 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
stands at 3.6 percent

04:29.936 --> 04:33.466 align:middle
in the fourth quarter of this
year and runs at 3.5 percent

04:33.636 --> 04:34.846 align:middle
over the next two years,

04:35.426 --> 04:38.406 align:middle
a percentage point below
the median estimate

04:38.406 --> 04:40.456 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

04:41.506 --> 04:44.526 align:middle
This median path is just a bit
lower than that from March.

04:46.486 --> 04:50.096 align:middle
After many years of running
below our 2 percent longer-run

04:50.096 --> 04:52.376 align:middle
objective, inflation
has recently moved close

04:52.376 --> 04:52.896 align:middle
to that level.

04:53.136 --> 04:56.346 align:middle
Indeed, overall consumer prices,
as measured by the price index

04:56.346 --> 04:57.966 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures,

04:58.476 --> 05:01.296 align:middle
increased 2 percent over the
12 months ending in April.

05:02.276 --> 05:05.636 align:middle
The core PCE index, which
excludes prices of energy

05:05.636 --> 05:07.636 align:middle
and food and tends to
be a better indicator

05:07.636 --> 05:11.876 align:middle
of future inflation, rose 1.8
percent over the same period.

05:12.886 --> 05:14.846 align:middle
As we had expected,
inflation moved

05:14.846 --> 05:18.856 align:middle
up as the unusually low
readings from last March dropped

05:18.856 --> 05:19.906 align:middle
out of the calculation.

05:20.476 --> 05:23.486 align:middle
The recent inflation data
have been encouraging,

05:23.786 --> 05:26.476 align:middle
but after many years of
inflation below our objective,

05:27.206 --> 05:28.866 align:middle
we do not want to
declare victory.

05:29.116 --> 05:32.876 align:middle
We want to ensure that inflation
remains near our symmetric 2

05:32.876 --> 05:35.646 align:middle
percent longer-run goal
on a sustained basis.

05:36.816 --> 05:39.196 align:middle
As we note in our
Statement of Longer-Run Goals

05:39.196 --> 05:42.516 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
the Committee would be concerned

05:42.566 --> 05:45.006 align:middle
if inflation were
running persistently above

05:45.006 --> 05:47.006 align:middle
or below our 2 percent
objective.

05:47.536 --> 05:51.736 align:middle
Of course, many factors affect
inflation-some temporary

05:51.736 --> 05:55.296 align:middle
and others more lasting-and at
any given time inflation may be

05:55.296 --> 05:56.856 align:middle
above or below 2 percent.

05:57.776 --> 05:59.216 align:middle
For example, the recent rise

05:59.216 --> 06:02.036 align:middle
in oil prices will likely
push inflation somewhat

06:02.036 --> 06:03.746 align:middle
above 2 percent in
coming months.

06:04.706 --> 06:07.236 align:middle
But that transitory development
should have little, if any,

06:07.236 --> 06:09.916 align:middle
consequence for inflation
over the next few years.

06:11.246 --> 06:14.136 align:middle
The median of participants'
projections for inflation runs

06:14.136 --> 06:16.406 align:middle
at 2.1 percent through 2020.

06:17.206 --> 06:18.956 align:middle
Relative to the March
projections,

06:18.956 --> 06:21.846 align:middle
the median inflation projection
is a little higher this year

06:21.886 --> 06:22.396 align:middle
and next.

06:22.996 --> 06:26.496 align:middle
As I mentioned, today
we took another step

06:26.496 --> 06:29.486 align:middle
in gradually scaling back
monetary policy accommodation

06:29.486 --> 06:31.846 align:middle
by raising the target range
for the federal funds rate

06:31.846 --> 06:33.066 align:middle
by 1/4 percentage point,

06:33.246 --> 06:35.316 align:middle
bringing it to 1
3/4 to 2 percent.

06:36.256 --> 06:38.436 align:middle
We also made some changes
to our policy statement,

06:38.436 --> 06:40.936 align:middle
reflecting that policy
normalization is proceeding

06:40.936 --> 06:42.496 align:middle
broadly as we have expected.

06:43.296 --> 06:47.206 align:middle
None of these changes signals
a change in our policy views.

06:47.716 --> 06:50.876 align:middle
For example, we removed
the language stating that

06:51.056 --> 06:54.186 align:middle
"the federal funds rate is
likely to remain, for some time,

06:54.186 --> 06:57.086 align:middle
below levels that are expected
to prevail in the longer run."

06:58.126 --> 07:00.326 align:middle
Since we introduced that
language a few years ago,

07:00.326 --> 07:01.506 align:middle
the economy has strengthened,

07:01.666 --> 07:04.786 align:middle
and the Committee has raised the
federal funds rate from near 0

07:05.166 --> 07:06.736 align:middle
to 1 3/4 to 2 percent.

07:07.766 --> 07:09.416 align:middle
As we continue to
note in our statement,

07:09.646 --> 07:12.726 align:middle
we expect to make further
gradual increases in that rate.

07:13.576 --> 07:16.126 align:middle
As a result, if the
economy evolves broadly

07:16.126 --> 07:18.546 align:middle
as we anticipate, the
federal funds rate will,

07:18.616 --> 07:21.736 align:middle
over the next year or so,
move well within the range

07:21.736 --> 07:23.656 align:middle
of estimates of the
normal long-run level.

07:24.606 --> 07:26.766 align:middle
Therefore, we thought that
now is an appropriate time

07:26.766 --> 07:29.856 align:middle
to remove this forward guidance
from our policy statement.

07:32.616 --> 07:34.716 align:middle
We continue to believe
that a gradual approach

07:34.716 --> 07:37.446 align:middle
for increasing the federal
funds rate will best promote a

07:37.446 --> 07:39.656 align:middle
sustained expansion
of economic activity,

07:40.116 --> 07:41.786 align:middle
strong labor market conditions,

07:42.376 --> 07:44.946 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent goal.

07:45.896 --> 07:49.056 align:middle
We are aware that raising rates
too slowly might raise the risk

07:49.056 --> 07:51.456 align:middle
that monetary policy would
need to tighten abruptly

07:51.546 --> 07:55.016 align:middle
down the road in response to
an unexpectedly sharp increase

07:55.016 --> 07:57.136 align:middle
in inflation or financial
excesses,

07:57.716 --> 07:59.486 align:middle
jeopardizing the
economic expansion.

08:00.716 --> 08:03.136 align:middle
Conversely, if we raise
interest rates too rapidly,

08:03.456 --> 08:06.046 align:middle
the economy could weaken,
and inflation could continue

08:06.046 --> 08:08.216 align:middle
to run persistently
below our objective.

08:08.756 --> 08:12.486 align:middle
The Committee's gradual
approach is reflected

08:12.486 --> 08:14.876 align:middle
in participants' projections
for the appropriate path

08:15.056 --> 08:16.136 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

08:17.006 --> 08:19.746 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 2.4

08:19.746 --> 08:24.096 align:middle
percent at the end of this year,
3.1 percent at the end of 2019,

08:24.436 --> 08:26.966 align:middle
and 3.4 percent at
the end of 2020.

08:28.066 --> 08:31.416 align:middle
By 2020, the median federal
funds rate is modestly

08:31.416 --> 08:33.316 align:middle
above its estimated
longer-run level.

08:34.496 --> 08:37.356 align:middle
These projections are very
similar to those made in March.

08:38.276 --> 08:40.946 align:middle
Although the median federal
funds rate edged up this year

08:40.946 --> 08:44.506 align:middle
and next, most participants did
not revise their projections.

08:45.776 --> 08:48.336 align:middle
I'll conclude by mentioning
two additional matters.

08:49.036 --> 08:52.236 align:middle
First, our program for
reducing our balance sheet,

08:52.366 --> 08:55.296 align:middle
which began in October,
is proceeding smoothly.

08:55.966 --> 08:58.056 align:middle
Barring a material and
unexpected weakening

08:58.056 --> 09:00.946 align:middle
in the outlook, this program
will proceed on schedule,

09:01.366 --> 09:03.116 align:middle
and our balance sheet
will continue to shrink.

09:04.506 --> 09:06.446 align:middle
As we have said,
changing the target range

09:06.446 --> 09:08.656 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is our primary means

09:08.656 --> 09:10.556 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy.

09:12.296 --> 09:16.476 align:middle
And, finally, as discussed in
the minutes of our May meeting,

09:17.016 --> 09:19.686 align:middle
we're making a small technical
adjustment in one of our tools

09:19.686 --> 09:21.206 align:middle
for implementing
monetary policy.

09:21.786 --> 09:24.396 align:middle
To keep the federal funds
rate in the target range,

09:24.396 --> 09:26.216 align:middle
we rely on the rate of interest

09:26.216 --> 09:28.686 align:middle
on excess reserves,
or the IOER rate.

09:29.306 --> 09:32.106 align:middle
Up until now, we have set
the IOER rate at the top

09:32.106 --> 09:33.896 align:middle
of the target range for
the federal funds rate.

09:34.576 --> 09:36.476 align:middle
In recent months, the
federal funds rate has moved

09:36.516 --> 09:38.096 align:middle
up toward the IOER rate

09:38.096 --> 09:40.546 align:middle
as short-term interest rates
have risen more generally.

09:41.086 --> 09:43.556 align:middle
So to move the federal funds
rate closer to the middle

09:43.556 --> 09:47.666 align:middle
of the target range, we are now
setting the IOER rate 5 basis

09:47.666 --> 09:50.006 align:middle
points below the upper
end of the target range.

09:50.696 --> 09:52.916 align:middle
This minor technical
adjustment has no bearing

09:53.016 --> 09:55.126 align:middle
on the appropriate path
for the federal funds rate

09:55.656 --> 09:57.566 align:middle
or financial conditions
more generally.

09:58.576 --> 10:00.916 align:middle
Thanks for listening, and I'll
be happy to take your questions.

10:00.956 --> 10:03.506 align:middle
JIM TANKERSLEY.

10:05.516 --> 10:06.366 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

10:06.366 --> 10:07.796 align:middle
Jim Tankersley, New York Times.

10:08.256 --> 10:10.206 align:middle
I have a question
about inflation

10:10.206 --> 10:11.366 align:middle
and a question about growth.

10:11.756 --> 10:14.776 align:middle
On inflation, I'm curious if
there's anything that's happened

10:14.806 --> 10:18.456 align:middle
since March that has changed
your assessment of the risk

10:18.456 --> 10:21.826 align:middle
of inflation increases
beyond what you forecast

10:22.046 --> 10:23.576 align:middle
in the year to come.

10:24.026 --> 10:26.406 align:middle
And, on growth, you mentioned
fiscal policy is adding

10:26.406 --> 10:27.906 align:middle
to growth, and I'm
curious if you could break

10:27.906 --> 10:29.726 align:middle
that down a little
bit further for us

10:29.726 --> 10:32.606 align:middle
and say-what effects do you
think the recent tax cuts are

10:32.606 --> 10:33.726 align:middle
having on growth?

10:34.036 --> 10:34.776 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

10:34.966 --> 10:38.366 align:middle
Sure. So since-I wouldn't
say anything has happened

10:38.366 --> 10:41.886 align:middle
since March to really change the
way I'm thinking about inflation

10:41.886 --> 10:43.536 align:middle
or the way the Committee's
thinking about inflation.

10:44.236 --> 10:46.896 align:middle
We've seen inflation
move very gradually

10:46.896 --> 10:48.636 align:middle
up toward our 2 percent
objective.

10:49.316 --> 10:53.766 align:middle
And part of that has been just
idiosyncratic things dropping

10:53.766 --> 10:56.606 align:middle
out from last March, which
were holding inflation-measured

10:56.606 --> 10:57.326 align:middle
inflation-down.

10:57.886 --> 10:59.816 align:middle
Part of it is just that
continued tightening

10:59.816 --> 11:02.296 align:middle
in the labor market and the
economy more broadly is pushing

11:02.296 --> 11:02.846 align:middle
inflation up.

11:02.846 --> 11:06.016 align:middle
So we continue to think, and the
Committee continues to think,

11:06.016 --> 11:09.776 align:middle
that we are just about at
our 2 percent goal but,

11:09.866 --> 11:14.166 align:middle
as I mentioned, not ready to
declare victory until we sustain

11:14.166 --> 11:17.346 align:middle
that over time, which
we haven't done yet.

11:17.346 --> 11:21.046 align:middle
You also asked about fiscal
policy, and there's a range

11:21.046 --> 11:23.176 align:middle
of views on the Committee
and, I think more broadly,

11:23.176 --> 11:26.046 align:middle
a range of views among
economists generally.

11:26.646 --> 11:29.906 align:middle
But I can say that the
Committee members-Committee

11:29.906 --> 11:34.256 align:middle
participants-generally believe
that the fiscal changes-and

11:34.256 --> 11:36.926 align:middle
that includes both the tax
cuts, individual and corporate,

11:36.926 --> 11:40.506 align:middle
and the spending changes-will
provide meaningful support

11:40.876 --> 11:43.956 align:middle
to demand, significant support
to demand over the course

11:43.956 --> 11:44.976 align:middle
of the next three years.

11:45.836 --> 11:48.396 align:middle
And the question-the
other question is,

11:48.396 --> 11:49.596 align:middle
what about the supply side?

11:49.596 --> 11:55.296 align:middle
So it is-it makes sense that if
you lower corporate tax rates

11:55.296 --> 11:57.716 align:middle
and allow faster
expensing of investment,

11:57.716 --> 11:59.136 align:middle
you will encourage
greater investment.

11:59.416 --> 12:00.836 align:middle
That should drive productivity.

12:01.126 --> 12:02.666 align:middle
That should increase
potential output.

12:02.996 --> 12:05.906 align:middle
So that really ought
to happen as well.

12:05.906 --> 12:08.666 align:middle
I think the amounts and
the timing of that coming

12:08.666 --> 12:11.016 align:middle
in are also quite uncertain.

12:11.326 --> 12:12.856 align:middle
There's also the possibility

12:12.856 --> 12:15.126 align:middle
that there would be
more labor supply

12:15.126 --> 12:19.286 align:middle
from lower individual tax rates,
again, in amounts and in timing

12:19.286 --> 12:20.356 align:middle
that might be more uncertain.

12:20.416 --> 12:23.166 align:middle
So that's how the Committee
generally is thinking

12:23.166 --> 12:24.376 align:middle
about-about fiscal policy.

12:26.046 --> 12:28.556 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:29.486 --> 12:31.736 align:middle
Thanks. Nick Timiraos,
the Wall Street Journal.

12:32.286 --> 12:35.476 align:middle
So the Fed is about four
interest rate increases,

12:35.476 --> 12:37.306 align:middle
using the projections
released today,

12:37.646 --> 12:40.656 align:middle
away from what might be
considered a neutral fed

12:40.656 --> 12:41.336 align:middle
funds rate.

12:41.706 --> 12:44.376 align:middle
And I wanted to ask how
you're thinking about what

12:44.406 --> 12:46.816 align:middle
to do once you get to neutral.

12:47.216 --> 12:49.836 align:middle
Under what conditions would
you decide, once you get there,

12:50.256 --> 12:52.946 align:middle
that it's okay to
stop raising rates?

12:53.326 --> 12:56.146 align:middle
And under what conditions
would you want to keep going?

12:57.646 --> 12:58.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

12:58.786 --> 13:03.236 align:middle
So for many, many years, we've
been far from maximum employment

13:03.236 --> 13:04.746 align:middle
and stable prices,
and so the need

13:04.746 --> 13:07.656 align:middle
for accommodative policy
has been-has been clear.

13:08.216 --> 13:09.926 align:middle
As the economy has strengthened

13:09.926 --> 13:11.906 align:middle
and as we've gradually
raised interest rates,

13:12.806 --> 13:14.816 align:middle
the question comes into view of,

13:14.896 --> 13:17.516 align:middle
how much longer will you
need to be accommodative?

13:17.676 --> 13:18.536 align:middle
And how will you know?

13:18.536 --> 13:22.166 align:middle
How will you know where-at what
point policy will be neutral?

13:22.166 --> 13:25.086 align:middle
Neutral meaning that interest
rates are neither pushing the

13:25.116 --> 13:28.416 align:middle
economy up nor trying
to restrain it.

13:28.596 --> 13:31.106 align:middle
So we know that we're getting
closer to that neutral level.

13:31.106 --> 13:33.826 align:middle
We don't have an exact
sense of how that will be.

13:33.826 --> 13:37.026 align:middle
So the Committee is
discussing, very actively,

13:37.026 --> 13:38.436 align:middle
the questions that you raise.

13:38.946 --> 13:41.746 align:middle
And, really, it boils
down to a question of,

13:41.746 --> 13:43.136 align:middle
what is appropriate policy?

13:43.136 --> 13:44.886 align:middle
And, you know, I-you
asked, how will we know?

13:44.886 --> 13:47.826 align:middle
So I think we'll be
very carefully looking

13:47.826 --> 13:51.086 align:middle
at incoming data on inflation,
on financial readings,

13:51.086 --> 13:54.856 align:middle
and on the labor market.

13:54.856 --> 13:56.316 align:middle
We have to acknowledge

13:56.366 --> 13:59.426 align:middle
that there are always
wide uncertainty bands

13:59.466 --> 14:01.576 align:middle
around the level
of, for example,

14:01.576 --> 14:02.736 align:middle
the natural rate
of unemployment.

14:02.736 --> 14:04.636 align:middle
But also, what is the
neutral rate of interest?

14:04.636 --> 14:06.996 align:middle
What is that rate of interest
that pushes neither up nor down?

14:07.446 --> 14:11.456 align:middle
So I think we-we'll be guided
by incoming data on the economy

14:12.036 --> 14:15.476 align:middle
and try to keep our minds
open as we move forward.

14:18.946 --> 14:20.846 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

14:21.106 --> 14:22.326 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

14:22.726 --> 14:28.086 align:middle
2.1 percent above target for two
and a half years starts to feel

14:28.086 --> 14:29.786 align:middle
like some of the
alternate frameworks

14:29.786 --> 14:32.396 align:middle
that have been discussed here,
be it price-level targeting

14:32.436 --> 14:36.676 align:middle
or trying to set expectations
higher so that you hit your 2.

14:36.676 --> 14:40.266 align:middle
In deciding how symmetric
is too symmetric,

14:40.266 --> 14:43.576 align:middle
what sort of parameters are
you using on that front?

14:43.576 --> 14:44.936 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

14:44.936 --> 14:48.666 align:middle
You know, the-our target
for-our medium-term objective

14:48.666 --> 14:50.936 align:middle
for inflation is 2
percent PCE inflation.

14:51.326 --> 14:55.256 align:middle
We feel that that target
has served the economy well,

14:56.116 --> 14:57.776 align:middle
and I'm strongly
committed to it.

14:57.776 --> 14:59.936 align:middle
The Committee is
strongly committed to it.

15:00.156 --> 15:02.496 align:middle
The sort of barriers to
making a material change

15:02.496 --> 15:04.506 align:middle
to that would be-would be
very high because, again,

15:04.506 --> 15:06.736 align:middle
we think it's fundamental,
and we think it's worked.

15:06.736 --> 15:10.156 align:middle
You asked about price-level
targeting

15:10.156 --> 15:12.186 align:middle
and that sort of thing.

15:12.186 --> 15:16.506 align:middle
You know, there are some ideas
that sort of take cognizance

15:16.506 --> 15:17.856 align:middle
of the fact that
rates are lower,

15:17.856 --> 15:20.236 align:middle
we're near the zero lower bound,

15:20.236 --> 15:22.966 align:middle
and that could put
downward pressure

15:23.056 --> 15:25.056 align:middle
on inflation expectations
if we're going to be

15:25.056 --> 15:27.446 align:middle
down at the zero lower
bound and, therefore,

15:27.446 --> 15:28.686 align:middle
sort of undermine
the credibility

15:28.686 --> 15:30.126 align:middle
of the 2 percent
inflation objective.

15:30.126 --> 15:32.476 align:middle
So the idea is to
have kind of a makeup.

15:32.836 --> 15:34.426 align:middle
If you-if you're below
target for a while,

15:34.426 --> 15:36.436 align:middle
you have a-you have a time
of being above target.

15:36.436 --> 15:38.906 align:middle
And the idea is to
enhance the credibility

15:38.906 --> 15:41.536 align:middle
of that 2 percent-that
2 percent target.

15:41.766 --> 15:44.216 align:middle
This is an idea that's been
written about for many years.

15:44.726 --> 15:46.976 align:middle
It's not something that
the Committee has looked

15:46.976 --> 15:47.706 align:middle
at seriously.

15:47.736 --> 15:50.676 align:middle
I imagine we will be
having discussions about it,

15:50.836 --> 15:54.646 align:middle
but-not something that we have
on the calendar right now.

15:57.576 --> 15:58.366 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

15:58.606 --> 15:59.346 align:middle
Well, thanks very much.

15:59.346 --> 16:00.666 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

16:01.066 --> 16:03.546 align:middle
Over the weekend, we saw
some significant tensions

16:03.546 --> 16:04.436 align:middle
within the G-7.

16:04.796 --> 16:07.836 align:middle
In Canada, there is the
potential, obviously,

16:07.926 --> 16:11.716 align:middle
for further action against China
right now and retaliatory action

16:11.816 --> 16:14.256 align:middle
from major U.S. trading
partners.

16:14.446 --> 16:17.406 align:middle
How big a risk do you
currently see this as being

16:17.406 --> 16:19.266 align:middle
to the United States economy?

16:19.326 --> 16:21.896 align:middle
And what kind of feedback
are you getting in terms

16:21.896 --> 16:24.006 align:middle
of corporate investment
intentions?

16:24.006 --> 16:25.056 align:middle
Is there something
that's beginning

16:25.056 --> 16:27.006 align:middle
to feature more prominently
in your own discussions

16:27.466 --> 16:28.896 align:middle
with major U.S. companies?

16:29.086 --> 16:31.036 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

16:31.036 --> 16:32.516 align:middle
I ought to start by
saying that, you know,

16:32.516 --> 16:35.826 align:middle
Congress has assigned us very
important jobs and-you know,

16:35.826 --> 16:38.116 align:middle
maximum employment, stable
prices, we have a role

16:38.116 --> 16:40.276 align:middle
in financial stability that
we share with other agencies.

16:40.716 --> 16:43.466 align:middle
Congress has very specifically
given authority over trade

16:43.466 --> 16:45.276 align:middle
to the executive branch,
so I wouldn't comment

16:45.276 --> 16:47.566 align:middle
on any particular
specific trade actions.

16:48.176 --> 16:50.346 align:middle
I will say that we, of course,

16:50.346 --> 16:53.806 align:middle
have-we have broad contacts
in-among business leaders

16:53.806 --> 16:54.496 align:middle
around the country.

16:55.206 --> 16:57.506 align:middle
And the Reserve Bank presidents,
in particular, have that.

16:57.506 --> 17:00.906 align:middle
And so they report in
the Beige Book and then

17:00.906 --> 17:02.476 align:middle
in person at the FOMC meeting.

17:02.476 --> 17:06.156 align:middle
And they do come back and they
say that concerns about changes

17:06.156 --> 17:10.486 align:middle
in trade policy are arising,
I think it's fair to say,

17:10.486 --> 17:12.496 align:middle
and also that you're
beginning to hear reports

17:12.566 --> 17:14.786 align:middle
of companies holding off

17:14.786 --> 17:16.426 align:middle
on making investments
and hiring people.

17:16.426 --> 17:20.186 align:middle
So right now we don't see
that in the numbers at all.

17:20.346 --> 17:21.876 align:middle
The economy is very strong.

17:22.336 --> 17:23.586 align:middle
The labor market is strong.

17:24.206 --> 17:25.046 align:middle
Growth is strong.

17:25.046 --> 17:26.966 align:middle
We really don't see
it in the numbers.

17:27.716 --> 17:29.016 align:middle
It's just not there.

17:29.326 --> 17:31.856 align:middle
But-so I would put it
down as more of a risk.

17:32.386 --> 17:34.856 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:38.296 --> 17:39.456 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

17:39.456 --> 17:41.446 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, you said
there's a difference

17:41.446 --> 17:43.766 align:middle
of opinion among economists,

17:43.766 --> 17:48.416 align:middle
but looking at the longer-run
GDP growth rates for the members

17:48.416 --> 17:50.306 align:middle
of the Committee, there's not
a whole lot of difference.

17:50.306 --> 17:55.206 align:middle
It's 1.8 to 2, or 1.7 to 2.1,
depending upon how you count it.

17:55.686 --> 17:59.366 align:middle
Is that showing us that
not a single member

17:59.366 --> 18:03.476 align:middle
of the Committee-including
yourself, Mr. Chairman-agrees

18:03.476 --> 18:05.646 align:middle
with economists over
at the White House

18:06.086 --> 18:10.166 align:middle
that they can achieve long-run
sustained growth rates above

18:10.166 --> 18:12.566 align:middle
or at 3 percent or higher?

18:12.566 --> 18:13.846 align:middle
Do you believe in that?

18:14.816 --> 18:15.606 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

18:15.606 --> 18:19.076 align:middle
You know, first of all, that's
a-that's a reasonable range,

18:19.076 --> 18:21.256 align:middle
I think, of-it's not that
we're all on the same number,

18:21.256 --> 18:23.646 align:middle
but there are a range of
views about potential growth.

18:24.146 --> 18:27.036 align:middle
And there's so much
uncertainty around this.

18:27.036 --> 18:30.116 align:middle
You know, we don't-the thing
about fiscal policy is,

18:30.276 --> 18:34.366 align:middle
you don't have thousands
of incidents to, you know,

18:34.366 --> 18:35.926 align:middle
to-you don't have
big data, in a way.

18:35.926 --> 18:36.976 align:middle
You have very small data.

18:36.976 --> 18:39.596 align:middle
You've got only a few instances
here, so you have a lot

18:39.596 --> 18:41.226 align:middle
of uncertainty around
what the effects will be.

18:41.556 --> 18:42.416 align:middle
They could be large.

18:42.526 --> 18:43.496 align:middle
We hope they're large.

18:43.956 --> 18:46.976 align:middle
But I think our approach is
going to be to watch and see

18:47.106 --> 18:51.286 align:middle
and hope that, in fact, we do
get significant effects to,

18:51.336 --> 18:54.426 align:middle
you know, to potential
growth out of the tax bill,

18:54.816 --> 18:56.496 align:middle
and we're just going
to have to see.

18:56.946 --> 18:59.836 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:59.836 --> 19:01.136 align:middle
How would you forecast
it though?

19:01.226 --> 19:01.626 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

19:01.626 --> 19:04.956 align:middle
I think we're looking at a
reasonable range of estimates

19:04.956 --> 19:06.896 align:middle
and we're putting
every-different participants are

19:06.896 --> 19:09.516 align:middle
putting different
estimates in and we're going

19:09.516 --> 19:14.896 align:middle
to be waiting and seeing.

19:15.316 --> 19:16.486 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

19:16.516 --> 19:18.496 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

19:19.056 --> 19:21.126 align:middle
You said earlier that it's
still a little too early

19:21.126 --> 19:23.126 align:middle
to declare victory on inflation.

19:23.256 --> 19:25.796 align:middle
I wanted to circle back on
a question that was asked

19:25.906 --> 19:31.446 align:middle
at the initial press conference
about what-what does the Fed say

19:31.446 --> 19:34.066 align:middle
in regards to the inflation
target as symmetric?

19:34.066 --> 19:36.606 align:middle
Like has the Committee given
any further thought in terms

19:36.656 --> 19:40.206 align:middle
of how comfortable it would be
rising above-whether it goes

19:40.206 --> 19:44.566 align:middle
higher than 2.1, if it reaches
2.2, 2.3, and for how long?

19:44.896 --> 19:45.896 align:middle
And now that you're planning

19:45.896 --> 19:48.706 align:middle
to hold these regular press
conferences, starting next year,

19:49.156 --> 19:51.046 align:middle
how do you explain-how
do you plan to explain

19:51.046 --> 19:53.786 align:middle
that to the American people that
inflation is not overrunning?

19:53.786 --> 19:54.636 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

19:54.996 --> 19:56.806 align:middle
You know, what we've
said in our Statement

19:56.806 --> 19:59.806 align:middle
of Longer-Run Principles and
Monetary Policy Strategy is

19:59.806 --> 20:02.056 align:middle
that the Committee would be
concerned if inflation were

20:02.056 --> 20:06.056 align:middle
to run persistently above or
below 2 percent, persistently

20:06.056 --> 20:07.076 align:middle
above or below 2 percent.

20:07.746 --> 20:09.646 align:middle
And that's what we
mean by symmetric.

20:09.866 --> 20:13.226 align:middle
We're looking at it equally on
either side and it's a matter

20:13.226 --> 20:14.226 align:middle
of persistent overruns.

20:14.226 --> 20:16.416 align:middle
We know that inflation is
going to bounce around.

20:16.416 --> 20:18.186 align:middle
For example, as I mentioned,

20:18.586 --> 20:20.226 align:middle
later this summer
there's a good chance

20:20.226 --> 20:22.866 align:middle
that headline inflation
will move up above 2 percent

20:22.866 --> 20:23.906 align:middle
because of oil prices.

20:24.766 --> 20:27.436 align:middle
Things buffet inflation
back and forth,

20:28.556 --> 20:31.336 align:middle
but-so we acknowledge that,
we understand that-and

20:31.336 --> 20:33.936 align:middle
if inflation were to
persistently run above

20:34.076 --> 20:38.736 align:middle
or below 2 percent, then we
would be using our tools to try

20:38.736 --> 20:41.326 align:middle
to move inflation back in
the direction of the target.

20:42.046 --> 20:44.856 align:middle
We do understand, though,
that we don't have the ability

20:44.856 --> 20:47.186 align:middle
to precisely hit that
target, so we expect

20:47.726 --> 20:49.516 align:middle
that inflation will
be above or below.

20:50.146 --> 20:52.936 align:middle
And we just hope that that
is-happens on a symmetric basis.

20:54.676 --> 20:56.436 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

20:57.946 --> 20:59.846 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

21:00.156 --> 21:04.306 align:middle
At this meeting, you
hiked your-the funds rate,

21:04.946 --> 21:09.156 align:middle
you changed the dot plot to
move from 3 to 4 for this year,

21:09.156 --> 21:13.136 align:middle
and you took out a sentence
that you'd been using for years

21:13.136 --> 21:15.356 align:middle
about how long rates
might stay low.

21:15.716 --> 21:17.746 align:middle
But you say that none
of this signals a change

21:17.746 --> 21:18.756 align:middle
in policy views.

21:18.816 --> 21:22.536 align:middle
But shouldn't we see from
this combination of things

21:22.536 --> 21:25.166 align:middle
that the Fed is moving
to tighter policy?

21:26.076 --> 21:26.606 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

21:26.826 --> 21:28.046 align:middle
I think what you should see is

21:28.096 --> 21:30.286 align:middle
that the economy is
continuing to make progress.

21:30.686 --> 21:34.116 align:middle
The economy has strengthened
so much since I joined the Fed,

21:34.956 --> 21:37.926 align:middle
you know, in 2012 and even
over the last couple of years.

21:37.926 --> 21:39.526 align:middle
The economy is in a
very different place.

21:39.756 --> 21:42.396 align:middle
We- unemployment was 10 percent
at the height of the crisis.

21:42.396 --> 21:44.906 align:middle
It's 3.8 percent now
and moving lower.

21:44.906 --> 21:49.426 align:middle
So really what you-the decision
you see today is another sign

21:49.566 --> 21:51.696 align:middle
that the U.S. economy
is in great shape.

21:52.266 --> 21:56.196 align:middle
Growth is strong, labor markets
are strong, inflation is close

21:56.196 --> 21:57.746 align:middle
to target, and that's
what you're seeing.

21:58.056 --> 21:58.806 align:middle
For many years,

21:58.806 --> 22:02.406 align:middle
as I mentioned-many years-we
had interest rates held low

22:03.116 --> 22:05.546 align:middle
to support economic activity.

22:06.156 --> 22:08.286 align:middle
And it's been clear that
as we've gotten closer

22:08.286 --> 22:11.316 align:middle
to our statutory goals, we
should normalize policy,

22:11.316 --> 22:13.986 align:middle
and that's really what we've
been consistently doing

22:13.986 --> 22:16.036 align:middle
for some years now.

22:16.926 --> 22:17.856 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

22:19.896 --> 22:20.506 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

22:20.506 --> 22:22.216 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

22:22.286 --> 22:23.896 align:middle
Can you give us an update

22:23.896 --> 22:26.396 align:middle
on what the FOMC
thinks about wages?

22:26.396 --> 22:29.136 align:middle
Are we finally going to see that
wage growth pickup this year?

22:29.136 --> 22:31.456 align:middle
I know you're forecasting a
little bit more inflation,

22:31.456 --> 22:34.016 align:middle
but is that going to translate
through to wage growth?

22:35.096 --> 22:35.806 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

22:36.126 --> 22:40.366 align:middle
You know, wages have
been gradually moving up.

22:40.536 --> 22:41.586 align:middle
Earlier in the recovery,

22:41.586 --> 22:46.076 align:middle
they were-there are many
different wage measures,

22:46.076 --> 22:48.356 align:middle
of course, but-so just-but
just to generalize,

22:48.356 --> 22:50.456 align:middle
wages were running
roughly around 2 percent

22:50.456 --> 22:53.846 align:middle
and they've moved gradually
up into between 2 to 3 percent

22:54.536 --> 22:57.916 align:middle
as the labor market has
become stronger and stronger.

22:58.786 --> 23:01.806 align:middle
I think it's fair
to say that some

23:02.206 --> 23:05.166 align:middle
of us-and I certainly would have
expected wages to react more

23:05.646 --> 23:08.556 align:middle
to the very significant
reduction in unemployment

23:08.556 --> 23:11.366 align:middle
that we've had, as I mentioned,
from 10 percent to 3.8 percent.

23:12.206 --> 23:15.266 align:middle
Part of that can be
explained by low productivity,

23:15.306 --> 23:16.276 align:middle
which is something we've talked

23:16.276 --> 23:18.126 align:middle
about at the Committee
and elsewhere.

23:18.826 --> 23:22.796 align:middle
But, nonetheless, I
think we had anticipated,

23:22.796 --> 23:24.916 align:middle
and many people have
anticipated, that wages-that

23:24.966 --> 23:26.916 align:middle
in a world where we're
hearing lots and lots

23:27.586 --> 23:29.866 align:middle
about labor shortages-everywhere
we go now,

23:29.866 --> 23:31.106 align:middle
we hear about labor
shortages-but

23:31.396 --> 23:32.706 align:middle
where is the wage reaction?

23:32.706 --> 23:34.026 align:middle
So it's a bit of a puzzle.

23:34.026 --> 23:37.346 align:middle
I wouldn't say it's a mystery,
but it's a bit of a puzzle.

23:37.346 --> 23:40.306 align:middle
And, frankly, I do think
there's a lot to like

23:40.516 --> 23:42.246 align:middle
about low unemployment.

23:43.166 --> 23:46.946 align:middle
And one of the things is-you
will see-pretty much people

23:46.946 --> 23:48.996 align:middle
who want to get jobs-not
everybody-but people who want

23:48.996 --> 23:50.806 align:middle
to get jobs, many of them
will be able to get jobs.

23:51.196 --> 23:52.346 align:middle
You will see wages go up.

23:52.696 --> 23:54.416 align:middle
You'll see people
at the, sort of,

23:54.416 --> 23:57.506 align:middle
the margins of the labor
force having an opportunity

23:57.506 --> 23:58.356 align:middle
to get back in work.

23:58.506 --> 23:59.466 align:middle
They benefit from that.

23:59.466 --> 24:00.686 align:middle
Society benefits from that.

24:00.686 --> 24:02.626 align:middle
So there are a lot of
things to really like,

24:02.856 --> 24:04.506 align:middle
including higher
wages, as you asked.

24:04.856 --> 24:07.736 align:middle
Our role, though, is
also to, you know,

24:07.786 --> 24:10.966 align:middle
to make sure that-that maximum
employment happens in a context

24:10.966 --> 24:12.536 align:middle
of price stability and
financial stability,

24:12.536 --> 24:14.856 align:middle
which is why we're
gradually raising rates.

24:15.396 --> 24:24.196 align:middle
DON LEE. Just a follow-up
on-Don Lee from the L.A. Times.

24:24.196 --> 24:27.906 align:middle
On both inflation
and unemployment,

24:27.906 --> 24:32.336 align:middle
the new projections-for
unemployment lower

24:32.336 --> 24:34.946 align:middle
than before and inflation
higher.

24:34.946 --> 24:40.396 align:middle
And how much is the Fed willing
to accept that's an overshoot

24:40.396 --> 24:42.616 align:middle
for both of those
before it affects policy?

24:43.676 --> 24:44.186 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

24:44.556 --> 24:48.386 align:middle
You mentioned that unemployment
moved down and inflation moved

24:48.386 --> 24:50.206 align:middle
up by truly small amounts.

24:50.206 --> 24:52.126 align:middle
If you look at the Summary
of Economic Projections,

24:52.206 --> 24:55.716 align:middle
things are moving by just
a tick or even a semitick

24:56.736 --> 24:58.756 align:middle
between now and March.

24:59.166 --> 25:00.506 align:middle
And you asked, you know-I mean,

25:00.506 --> 25:03.326 align:middle
I think we take a
longer-run view

25:03.906 --> 25:07.456 align:middle
that we're shooting
for-we're aiming for-2 percent

25:07.456 --> 25:09.676 align:middle
inflation-inflation
around 2 percent.

25:09.876 --> 25:11.286 align:middle
We know that it'll
be above or below.

25:11.956 --> 25:13.926 align:middle
We're not going to-we
didn't overreact, I think,

25:13.926 --> 25:15.496 align:middle
to inflation being
under 2 percent.

25:15.496 --> 25:18.566 align:middle
We won't overreact to
it being over 2 percent.

25:19.186 --> 25:20.866 align:middle
And I think we'll
always be using our tools

25:20.906 --> 25:23.416 align:middle
to move inflation in the
direction of the target,

25:23.586 --> 25:25.526 align:middle
if it-if it leaves-if
it moves away

25:25.526 --> 25:27.496 align:middle
from the target persistently,
as I mentioned.

25:28.356 --> 25:33.036 align:middle
In terms of unemployment, you
know, you have to acknowledge

25:33.036 --> 25:37.196 align:middle
that we are-no one really knows
with certainty what the level

25:37.196 --> 25:38.946 align:middle
of the natural rate
of unemployment is,

25:38.996 --> 25:41.866 align:middle
the rate that is sustainable
over a long period of time.

25:42.646 --> 25:45.696 align:middle
And we know that probably
that rate has declined

25:45.696 --> 25:48.256 align:middle
as the U.S. population
has become more educated,

25:48.256 --> 25:49.726 align:middle
as it has become older.

25:50.566 --> 25:53.436 align:middle
Older and more educated people
have lower unemployment rates.

25:53.636 --> 25:55.206 align:middle
We don't know this
with precision.

25:55.206 --> 25:57.306 align:middle
So we have to be
learning as we go.

25:57.306 --> 25:59.926 align:middle
We've got to be looking at data

26:00.066 --> 26:02.006 align:middle
and informed by what's
coming in.

26:02.006 --> 26:04.746 align:middle
And as I mentioned, I think
at the last press conference,

26:05.326 --> 26:10.276 align:middle
estimates by the-by members
of the Committee have moved

26:10.276 --> 26:14.646 align:middle
down by a full percentage
point since maybe 2012

26:14.746 --> 26:17.746 align:middle
as we've learned-as
unemployment has dropped

26:17.746 --> 26:19.406 align:middle
and inflation hasn't
really reacted.

26:19.406 --> 26:22.776 align:middle
So I can't give you a precise
number, but I just-you know,

26:22.776 --> 26:25.256 align:middle
we will be very much
informed by incoming data.

26:25.696 --> 26:29.306 align:middle
And this uncertainty is
why-the fact that we live

26:29.306 --> 26:32.006 align:middle
in that uncertainty is why we've
been gradually raising rates.

26:32.426 --> 26:34.846 align:middle
We're not waiting for
inflation to show up.

26:34.906 --> 26:38.376 align:middle
We're going ahead and moving
gradually and trying to navigate

26:38.406 --> 26:40.316 align:middle
between two risks, really.

26:40.346 --> 26:42.616 align:middle
One would be moving too
quickly-inflation never gets

26:42.656 --> 26:44.506 align:middle
back to target if we do that.

26:44.716 --> 26:47.836 align:middle
And the other is
moving too slowly,

26:48.026 --> 26:50.046 align:middle
and then we have-we
have too much inflation

26:50.046 --> 26:52.306 align:middle
or financial instability,
and we have to raise quickly.

26:52.836 --> 26:56.806 align:middle
And that can also
have bad outcomes.

26:59.656 --> 27:02.356 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

27:03.086 --> 27:04.566 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

27:04.566 --> 27:07.016 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I have
a couple questions

27:07.016 --> 27:09.906 align:middle
about the interest the Fed
pays on excess reserves.

27:10.316 --> 27:13.406 align:middle
And you mentioned, of course,
that-that the IOER was raised

27:13.406 --> 27:16.186 align:middle
by the Committee 20 basis
points, and that's a result,

27:16.186 --> 27:17.626 align:middle
as you said, of the upward drift

27:17.846 --> 27:20.816 align:middle
of the effective fed funds
rate in that target range.

27:21.306 --> 27:23.866 align:middle
Do you think that that's
going to resolve that issue

27:23.866 --> 27:26.466 align:middle
or might there be further
action required by the Committee

27:26.466 --> 27:29.966 align:middle
in the future to continue
lowering IOER relative

27:30.006 --> 27:31.456 align:middle
to the midpoint of the range?

27:32.016 --> 27:37.246 align:middle
And, further, was there
discussion among the Committee

27:37.246 --> 27:38.926 align:middle
today about what's causing that?

27:39.006 --> 27:42.856 align:middle
Is it purely technical, perhaps
related to bill issuance,

27:43.386 --> 27:48.016 align:middle
or is it telling you something
about the level of scarcity

27:48.016 --> 27:50.876 align:middle
and truly excess bank reserves?

27:50.986 --> 27:51.426 align:middle
Thank you.

27:52.246 --> 27:52.626 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

27:52.906 --> 27:57.026 align:middle
Thanks. So I would say
that-remember the important

27:57.026 --> 27:59.416 align:middle
thing is that we want
the federal funds rate

27:59.416 --> 28:00.736 align:middle
to trade in the target range.

28:01.006 --> 28:03.176 align:middle
That's the whole,
the whole idea.

28:03.176 --> 28:05.446 align:middle
IOER is the principal
tool by which we assure

28:05.876 --> 28:06.646 align:middle
that that will happen.

28:07.256 --> 28:10.906 align:middle
And we've said in our,
you know, basic documents

28:10.966 --> 28:14.436 align:middle
that we will adjust the use
of our tools, as appropriate.

28:15.466 --> 28:18.566 align:middle
We don't expect to have
to do this often or again,

28:19.136 --> 28:20.636 align:middle
but we're not sure about that.

28:20.636 --> 28:23.556 align:middle
If we have to do it
again, we'll do it again.

28:23.896 --> 28:25.096 align:middle
Again, don't expect
it to happen.

28:25.516 --> 28:26.266 align:middle
You asked why.

28:26.266 --> 28:29.216 align:middle
And yes, you know, we-of
course, we're looking carefully

28:29.246 --> 28:32.496 align:middle
at that and, you know, the
truth is we don't-we don't know

28:32.496 --> 28:33.336 align:middle
with any precision.

28:33.336 --> 28:34.386 align:middle
Really, no one does.

28:34.386 --> 28:36.696 align:middle
It's-you can't run
experiments, you know,

28:36.696 --> 28:38.826 align:middle
with one effect and
not the other.

28:39.276 --> 28:42.496 align:middle
You know, I think there's a
lot of probability on the idea

28:42.496 --> 28:45.866 align:middle
of just high bill supply
leads to higher repo costs,

28:45.866 --> 28:47.536 align:middle
higher money market
rates generally,

28:47.536 --> 28:50.876 align:middle
and the arbitrage pulls up
federal funds rate towards IOER.

28:51.566 --> 28:53.836 align:middle
We don't know that that's the
only effect and, you know,

28:53.836 --> 28:56.476 align:middle
we're just going to have to
be watching and learning.

28:56.826 --> 28:58.486 align:middle
And, frankly, we don't
have to know today.

28:58.516 --> 29:01.096 align:middle
What we really need is to have
the federal funds rate trade

29:01.216 --> 29:04.456 align:middle
in the range, and that's
what this minor technical

29:04.456 --> 29:05.796 align:middle
adjustment accomplishes.

29:06.236 --> 29:09.096 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

29:10.936 --> 29:12.516 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

29:12.516 --> 29:14.476 align:middle
So with the numbers
that we're looking at,

29:14.476 --> 29:16.366 align:middle
you talked about more
people getting jobs,

29:16.366 --> 29:17.616 align:middle
the wages are increasing.

29:18.056 --> 29:22.416 align:middle
Are we seeing a-with the fiscal
policy-a fundamental shift

29:22.556 --> 29:25.786 align:middle
in the economy, where we have
lower natural unemployment,

29:26.076 --> 29:29.056 align:middle
also possibly a lower rate

29:29.056 --> 29:33.626 align:middle
of natural unemployment
and lower inflation?

29:33.996 --> 29:34.786 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

29:36.936 --> 29:38.646 align:middle
Your question-your first
question, really, is,

29:38.646 --> 29:41.046 align:middle
do we think the natural rate
of unemployment is lower?

29:41.046 --> 29:44.936 align:middle
So I think we do believe it
has moved down significantly

29:44.936 --> 29:46.106 align:middle
over a long period of time.

29:47.026 --> 29:51.856 align:middle
We don't think that the natural
rate of unemployment-you know,

29:51.856 --> 29:53.886 align:middle
it's not one of those variables
that moves around a lot.

29:53.886 --> 29:57.216 align:middle
It tends to be driven by
slow-moving variables,

29:57.216 --> 30:00.086 align:middle
like the education level, the
population, like the functioning

30:00.086 --> 30:01.566 align:middle
of the labor market,
and things like that.

30:01.566 --> 30:06.076 align:middle
So, you know, it may-it
may have moved down too,

30:06.176 --> 30:09.546 align:middle
on a cyclical basis, lower.

30:09.746 --> 30:11.436 align:middle
As the economy gets
hotter and hotter,

30:11.436 --> 30:12.726 align:middle
there's some possibility
of that.

30:12.866 --> 30:15.126 align:middle
But, you know, the thing
is, if you look back,

30:15.126 --> 30:17.036 align:middle
there have been a lot of
studies done and, you know,

30:17.096 --> 30:19.136 align:middle
real-time estimates
of the natural rate

30:19.136 --> 30:21.386 align:middle
of unemployment have
uncertainty bands,

30:21.386 --> 30:25.106 align:middle
which are-which are quite wide,
so we have to remember that

30:25.226 --> 30:29.406 align:middle
and very much be guided by
the-by the incoming data.

30:29.916 --> 30:31.036 align:middle
You asked about inflation.

30:31.236 --> 30:33.516 align:middle
You know, inflation
we look about-we look

30:33.516 --> 30:36.416 align:middle
at the 2 percent inflation
objective as something

30:36.416 --> 30:39.166 align:middle
that central banks, the
Fed, really control.

30:39.376 --> 30:41.606 align:middle
And we have to be strongly
committed to achieving

30:41.606 --> 30:42.966 align:middle
that using our tools to do that.

30:43.666 --> 30:47.006 align:middle
I think in-in recent years
the dominant force has been,

30:47.396 --> 30:50.746 align:middle
you know, disinflationary-have
been pushing down on inflation.

30:50.746 --> 30:52.006 align:middle
And so we've been
pushing back up.

30:52.456 --> 30:53.856 align:middle
Of course, all those years

30:53.856 --> 30:55.356 align:middle
when we were growing
up, it was the opposite.

30:55.356 --> 30:56.926 align:middle
Inflation was too high

30:57.376 --> 31:00.476 align:middle
and central banks were
constantly pushing down.

31:00.566 --> 31:02.126 align:middle
It's really important

31:02.126 --> 31:04.686 align:middle
that inflation not
fall below 2 percent,

31:04.686 --> 31:06.996 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remain well anchored

31:07.096 --> 31:09.946 align:middle
at 2 percent-very
important-because the

31:09.946 --> 31:13.696 align:middle
implications of inflation below
2 percent are that you're closer

31:13.696 --> 31:16.376 align:middle
to the zero lower bound, meaning
the Fed has less room to cut,

31:16.926 --> 31:19.066 align:middle
meaning that we'll spend more
time there and we won't be able

31:19.066 --> 31:22.166 align:middle
to do the job that we're
assigned to do for our citizens.

31:22.986 --> 31:25.566 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

31:26.566 --> 31:31.266 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
Bloomberg Television.

31:31.666 --> 31:36.666 align:middle
You guys moved the median
unemployment forecast for 2020

31:36.666 --> 31:40.496 align:middle
down to 3.5 percent but left the
longer-run at 4.5 percent today.

31:40.776 --> 31:43.346 align:middle
But you're only forecasting
a moderate overshoot

31:43.346 --> 31:46.236 align:middle
on the fed funds rate beyond
your longer-run value.

31:46.376 --> 31:49.666 align:middle
How are you going to get
unemployment from 3.5 percent

31:49.666 --> 31:51.076 align:middle
up to that 4.5 percent rate?

31:51.076 --> 31:52.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

31:52.486 --> 31:54.566 align:middle
I would just-would-I
would just emphasize,

31:54.566 --> 31:56.886 align:middle
emphasize that-a couple things.

31:56.886 --> 31:59.556 align:middle
First, we're learning
about the real location

31:59.556 --> 32:01.336 align:middle
of the natural rate of
unemployment as we go.

32:01.486 --> 32:05.516 align:middle
So it's moved down by more

32:05.516 --> 32:07.486 align:middle
than a full percentage
point since 2012.

32:07.486 --> 32:10.356 align:middle
So it's not so simple as
thinking, oh boy, we've just got

32:10.356 --> 32:11.686 align:middle
to go ahead and get
that rate up.

32:11.686 --> 32:14.526 align:middle
If you-if you look at the
forecast, two years from now,

32:14.806 --> 32:18.096 align:middle
end of 2020, you're still seeing
inflation very close to target.

32:18.756 --> 32:21.536 align:middle
So there's no sense that
inflation will-no sense

32:21.536 --> 32:22.996 align:middle
in our models, or
in our projections,

32:22.996 --> 32:25.266 align:middle
or forecasts-that
inflation will take off

32:25.946 --> 32:29.446 align:middle
or move unexpectedly
quickly from these levels,

32:29.446 --> 32:31.466 align:middle
even if unemployment
does remain low.

32:32.276 --> 32:36.396 align:middle
So that's-that's what-so
it's important to know

32:36.396 --> 32:38.556 align:middle
that the-the unemployment
rate forecasts go

32:38.556 --> 32:41.546 align:middle
with the inflation forecasts
and go with the rate forecasts.

32:41.956 --> 32:45.156 align:middle
And so each person who's
submitting them is submitting

32:45.676 --> 32:47.986 align:middle
accommodate-you know,
appropriate monetary policy

32:48.196 --> 32:49.976 align:middle
that fits with that
person's assessment.

32:49.976 --> 32:51.456 align:middle
And their assessments
generally are

32:51.916 --> 32:53.326 align:middle
to support maximum employment

32:53.446 --> 32:55.766 align:middle
and stable prices
around 2 percent.

32:55.766 --> 32:58.356 align:middle
So if we thought that inflation
were going to take off,

32:58.356 --> 33:00.146 align:middle
obviously, we'd be
showing higher rates,

33:00.236 --> 33:01.566 align:middle
but that's not what
we think will happen.

33:02.126 --> 33:04.146 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

33:04.546 --> 33:06.286 align:middle
If I could just follow
up really quickly.

33:06.286 --> 33:09.736 align:middle
Then why, I guess, would the
longer-run unemployment rate not

33:09.736 --> 33:12.976 align:middle
be a little bit lower and
closer to that 2020 number?

33:13.506 --> 33:13.986 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

33:14.026 --> 33:15.076 align:middle
Yeah, it may be.

33:15.586 --> 33:16.026 align:middle
It may be.

33:16.026 --> 33:18.436 align:middle
We may find that out.

33:18.636 --> 33:19.856 align:middle
You know, the best
estimate that we have,

33:19.856 --> 33:22.156 align:middle
over the longer run, is that.

33:22.156 --> 33:23.856 align:middle
Although, you know, there's
a range of views, you know.

33:23.856 --> 33:25.846 align:middle
Some people are in the
low 4s, and, again,

33:25.846 --> 33:28.236 align:middle
I said the uncertainty
bands are, you know,

33:28.336 --> 33:30.486 align:middle
not quite a full percentage
point on either side,

33:30.486 --> 33:33.596 align:middle
but 3/4 of a percent, that kind
of thing, so it's very possible.

33:33.596 --> 33:39.676 align:middle
We have to be, you know-we can't
do-we can't be too attached

33:39.676 --> 33:41.336 align:middle
to these unobservable variables.

33:41.466 --> 33:43.106 align:middle
You know, we-I think
we have to be practical

33:43.106 --> 33:46.186 align:middle
about the way we think
about these things and we do

33:46.186 --> 33:47.986 align:middle
that by being grounded
in the data

33:48.136 --> 33:50.226 align:middle
and what we see happening
in the real economy.

33:52.036 --> 33:52.486 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

33:52.596 --> 33:58.146 align:middle
I have a couple of
regulatory questions.

33:58.406 --> 34:01.026 align:middle
First of all, on the
countercyclical capital buffer,

34:01.066 --> 34:03.896 align:middle
I was wondering, what are the
chances that the Fed is going

34:03.896 --> 34:06.276 align:middle
to need to use that in
the next year or two?

34:06.686 --> 34:09.536 align:middle
And then my second question
is, there's been a lot

34:09.656 --> 34:12.426 align:middle
of talk lately in Congress
about the ability for banks

34:12.426 --> 34:15.286 align:middle
to serve marijuana businesses,
and I was wondering if you think

34:15.286 --> 34:17.996 align:middle
that banks should be able to
serve those businesses in states

34:17.996 --> 34:19.386 align:middle
where marijuana is legal.

34:20.396 --> 34:20.786 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

34:20.786 --> 34:24.026 align:middle
So the countercyclical capital
buffer gives us the ability

34:24.026 --> 34:26.796 align:middle
to raise capital requirements
on the largest institutions

34:26.866 --> 34:31.616 align:middle
when financial stability
vulnerabilities are meaningfully

34:31.616 --> 34:32.266 align:middle
above normal.

34:32.266 --> 34:34.046 align:middle
That's the language
that we've used.

34:34.196 --> 34:35.946 align:middle
And that's certainly
a possibility.

34:35.946 --> 34:38.006 align:middle
I wouldn't say that-I
wouldn't look

34:38.006 --> 34:41.296 align:middle
at today's financial
stability landscape and say

34:41.296 --> 34:43.656 align:middle
that risks are meaningfully
above normal.

34:43.766 --> 34:45.956 align:middle
I would say that they're
roughly at normal.

34:46.426 --> 34:48.996 align:middle
You have-you know,
households are well-you know,

34:48.996 --> 34:49.906 align:middle
are in good shape.

34:50.056 --> 34:52.226 align:middle
They're-they've paid
down their debt,

34:52.906 --> 34:54.846 align:middle
incomes are rising,
people have jobs.

34:54.846 --> 34:56.786 align:middle
So households are
not really a concern.

34:57.416 --> 34:59.386 align:middle
And banks are highly
capitalized,

34:59.386 --> 35:00.636 align:middle
so that's not really a concern.

35:00.636 --> 35:02.956 align:middle
We see-there's some
concern with asset prices

35:02.956 --> 35:04.166 align:middle
in a couple of pockets.

35:04.546 --> 35:07.136 align:middle
But overall, if you-if you
bake it all in, I think we see,

35:07.616 --> 35:11.136 align:middle
generally, financial
vulnerabilities as moderate.

35:11.266 --> 35:13.126 align:middle
Could that change, you asked,
over a couple of years?

35:13.246 --> 35:15.316 align:middle
Yeah, it could.

35:15.526 --> 35:19.126 align:middle
You also asked about
marijuana businesses.

35:19.126 --> 35:21.146 align:middle
So this is a very difficult area

35:21.146 --> 35:25.616 align:middle
because we have state law-many
state laws permit the use

35:25.856 --> 35:28.936 align:middle
of marijuana and federal
law still doesn't.

35:28.936 --> 35:31.416 align:middle
So it puts, you know,
federally chartered banks

35:31.416 --> 35:32.936 align:middle
in a very difficult situation.

35:32.936 --> 35:34.876 align:middle
I think it would be great
if that could be clarified.

35:35.396 --> 35:39.036 align:middle
We don't have-you know, it
puts the supervisor in a very,

35:39.036 --> 35:39.986 align:middle
very difficult position.

35:40.416 --> 35:44.036 align:middle
And, of course, this isn't
our-our mandate has nothing

35:44.036 --> 35:49.466 align:middle
to do with marijuana, so we
don't really-we just would love

35:49.466 --> 35:50.636 align:middle
to see it clarified, I think.

35:51.456 --> 35:53.216 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

35:55.026 --> 35:56.776 align:middle
Hi, John Heltman
with American Banker.

35:56.856 --> 36:01.096 align:middle
So since you-since even before
you were Chairman of the Fed,

36:01.096 --> 36:03.656 align:middle
when you were chair of
the supervisory committee,

36:03.656 --> 36:05.316 align:middle
you laid out a sort

36:05.656 --> 36:08.426 align:middle
of regulatory revision
agenda that's actually been

36:08.426 --> 36:09.226 align:middle
pretty consistent.

36:09.226 --> 36:12.476 align:middle
So there was the guidance
on boards of governors,

36:12.926 --> 36:15.306 align:middle
there was the-some changes
to the stress tests,

36:15.396 --> 36:19.686 align:middle
and-not changes to the stress
test, but rather clarification

36:19.686 --> 36:23.536 align:middle
on the modeling-and
now, more recently,

36:23.536 --> 36:26.186 align:middle
the changes to the enhanced
supplemental leverage ratio.

36:26.736 --> 36:30.976 align:middle
The Fed has also proposed some
changes to the Volcker rule and,

36:30.976 --> 36:33.866 align:middle
as I mentioned a minute ago,
changes to the stress test

36:33.866 --> 36:35.206 align:middle
with the stress capital buffer.

36:35.786 --> 36:39.756 align:middle
Are these kind of the-are
there any new frontiers

36:39.756 --> 36:43.456 align:middle
of regulatory changes
that you are envisioning

36:43.456 --> 36:46.836 align:middle
or are you just-are you kind
of done for the time being?

36:46.836 --> 36:50.276 align:middle
Or what else can we
expect from the Fed?

36:50.276 --> 36:50.706 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

36:51.056 --> 36:52.646 align:middle
It's actually a pretty
full docket right now.

36:52.646 --> 36:54.206 align:middle
You mentioned a number
of the things,

36:54.206 --> 36:55.796 align:middle
but I would-I would point
out-we're having, I guess,

36:55.796 --> 36:58.246 align:middle
a public Board meeting tomorrow

36:58.246 --> 37:01.486 align:middle
on the single counterparty
credit limit provision.

37:02.126 --> 37:05.026 align:middle
We've also got quite
a lot of work

37:05.396 --> 37:11.166 align:middle
to promulgate rules
to-after S. 2155

37:11.166 --> 37:13.486 align:middle
that Senator Crapo's bill
passed-we've got a lot

37:13.486 --> 37:14.366 align:middle
of work to do under that.

37:14.366 --> 37:16.586 align:middle
We've got to think about
how we would reach below

37:16.586 --> 37:22.506 align:middle
that $250 billion threshold to
assess and supervise, regulate,

37:22.916 --> 37:25.076 align:middle
you know, financial stability
risks below that level.

37:25.696 --> 37:27.006 align:middle
So what am I missing?

37:27.006 --> 37:30.306 align:middle
There's-there-oh, oh, net
stable funding ratio is

37:30.306 --> 37:31.116 align:middle
out there to be done.

37:31.656 --> 37:33.386 align:middle
So there's a lot of
work to do, I think.

37:33.716 --> 37:35.976 align:middle
You know, and if I can just
take this opportunity to say,

37:36.646 --> 37:40.386 align:middle
you know, the financial system
all but failed 10 years ago.

37:40.546 --> 37:44.446 align:middle
We went to work for 10 years to
strengthen it-stronger capital,

37:44.446 --> 37:47.966 align:middle
stronger liquidity, stress
testing, resolution planning.

37:48.076 --> 37:49.246 align:middle
We want to keep all that stuff.

37:49.246 --> 37:52.416 align:middle
We want to make it, you
know, even more effective

37:52.416 --> 37:53.576 align:middle
and certainly more efficient.

37:53.636 --> 37:56.436 align:middle
We want to tailor those
regulations for institutions.

37:56.506 --> 37:58.376 align:middle
We want the strongest
provisions to apply

37:58.376 --> 38:00.736 align:middle
to the most systemically
important institutions.

38:00.736 --> 38:03.186 align:middle
And so we're committed
to preserving

38:03.186 --> 38:04.876 align:middle
and enhancing that structure.

38:05.236 --> 38:07.826 align:middle
But we-we're finding a lot
that we can do in the way

38:07.826 --> 38:09.806 align:middle
of tailoring regulations
for the smaller,

38:10.206 --> 38:12.286 align:middle
less systemically important
institutions, and that's a lot

38:12.286 --> 38:13.196 align:middle
of what we're working
on right now.

38:14.336 --> 38:15.206 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

38:17.436 --> 38:18.166 align:middle
Thank you.

38:19.176 --> 38:21.396 align:middle
You said at the beginning
of your press conference

38:21.396 --> 38:24.186 align:middle
that you plan to be
more plainspoken.

38:24.856 --> 38:27.986 align:middle
And so, okay, I wanted to know
what you would say to workers

38:28.486 --> 38:31.356 align:middle
who are worried that, you
know, these paths of rate hikes

38:31.356 --> 38:33.056 align:middle
that you've laid out will kind

38:33.056 --> 38:37.166 align:middle
of undercut the wage growth
they are just starting to see.

38:37.486 --> 38:37.766 align:middle
Thank you.

38:37.766 --> 38:38.856 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

38:38.976 --> 38:41.276 align:middle
You know, I would say that
the economy is in great shape.

38:41.656 --> 38:44.966 align:middle
If you look at household
surveys, confidence is high.

38:44.966 --> 38:47.326 align:middle
Look at businesses,
confidence is high.

38:47.846 --> 38:52.556 align:middle
If you ask-if you survey
workers about the job market,

38:52.556 --> 38:55.296 align:middle
they'll say that it's a really
good environment to find jobs.

38:55.486 --> 38:56.586 align:middle
If you survey businesses,

38:56.616 --> 38:58.516 align:middle
they'll say that
workers are scarce.

38:58.936 --> 39:03.396 align:middle
So I think overall, we have-we
have a really solid economy

39:03.396 --> 39:04.366 align:middle
on our hands here.

39:04.436 --> 39:07.766 align:middle
And so what we're
doing is, we are trying

39:07.766 --> 39:10.566 align:middle
to conduct monetary policy
in a way that will sustain

39:10.566 --> 39:13.016 align:middle
that expansion, keep the
labor markets strong,

39:13.016 --> 39:16.516 align:middle
and keep inflation
above-right at-sorry, not above,

39:16.616 --> 39:17.976 align:middle
but right at 2 percent.

39:18.546 --> 39:21.646 align:middle
That's really what we're
trying to do and, you know,

39:21.646 --> 39:24.096 align:middle
I would say I like
the results so far.

39:25.006 --> 39:27.596 align:middle
We're-we've been
very, very careful not

39:27.956 --> 39:28.866 align:middle
to tighten too quickly.

39:29.456 --> 39:30.556 align:middle
I think we've been patient.

39:30.556 --> 39:32.856 align:middle
I think that patience
has borne fruit

39:32.856 --> 39:33.986 align:middle
and I think it continues to.

39:34.416 --> 39:36.366 align:middle
We had a lot of encouragement
to go much faster

39:37.226 --> 39:38.236 align:middle
and I'm really glad we didn't.

39:39.026 --> 39:41.216 align:middle
But, at this time,
the-continuing

39:41.216 --> 39:45.226 align:middle
on that gradual pace
seems-continues

39:45.226 --> 39:46.916 align:middle
to seem like the right thing.

39:47.026 --> 39:50.546 align:middle
If we get a sense that the
economy is reacting badly,

39:50.546 --> 39:56.466 align:middle
then we'll certainly
react to that.

39:57.196 --> 39:58.946 align:middle
DAVID HARRISON.

39:59.486 --> 40:02.046 align:middle
Hi, David Harrison with
Dow Jones Newswires.

40:03.416 --> 40:06.376 align:middle
Where do you see the neutral
interest rate is right now?

40:06.376 --> 40:09.116 align:middle
Do you think it's-do you see
it sort of inching up because

40:09.166 --> 40:12.156 align:middle
of the recent fiscal
stimulus measures?

40:12.196 --> 40:16.476 align:middle
And how will you know
when we're getting close

40:16.476 --> 40:18.006 align:middle
to that neutral point?

40:18.116 --> 40:21.746 align:middle
So if-you know, if inflation
stays around 2, it doesn't go

40:21.746 --> 40:24.196 align:middle
above 2 for a while,
do you see a need

40:24.196 --> 40:26.326 align:middle
to actually exceed
that neutral point?

40:26.946 --> 40:28.076 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

40:28.416 --> 40:31.086 align:middle
So I would just point you
to the range of estimates

40:31.086 --> 40:35.906 align:middle
at the Committee, which I
think is 2 1/4 to 3 1/2,

40:35.906 --> 40:38.496 align:middle
and the median is
2.9, right in there.

40:38.496 --> 40:41.106 align:middle
So that's the range of estimates

40:41.226 --> 40:44.236 align:middle
of the nominal neutral
rate of interest.

40:44.526 --> 40:46.626 align:middle
And we do understand that
there's high uncertainty

40:46.626 --> 40:48.966 align:middle
around the level, but that's
kind of-so you can think of 2.9

40:48.966 --> 40:52.526 align:middle
as being-which is sort of a
full percentage point away

40:52.526 --> 40:57.906 align:middle
from where fed funds is going
to trade after today's decision.

40:58.116 --> 41:00.736 align:middle
You asked, is the-is
the neutral rate moving

41:00.736 --> 41:01.956 align:middle
up because of fiscal policy?

41:02.076 --> 41:04.056 align:middle
Yes. I mean, there's-there
should be an effect

41:04.056 --> 41:05.436 align:middle
if you have increased deficits

41:05.436 --> 41:08.976 align:middle
that should put upward
pressure on, you know,

41:08.976 --> 41:10.066 align:middle
a few tenths, let's say.

41:10.616 --> 41:13.026 align:middle
Again, though, they were
estimating these things.

41:13.026 --> 41:14.516 align:middle
It's one of these
unobserved variables

41:14.516 --> 41:17.116 align:middle
so it's very hard to-we
shouldn't try to speak about it

41:17.116 --> 41:18.726 align:middle
with a-with a lot of
precision or confidence.

41:18.726 --> 41:22.546 align:middle
But, yes, that should put
upward pressure on it.

41:22.546 --> 41:23.306 align:middle
How will we know?

41:23.386 --> 41:25.256 align:middle
Well, I think you have
to look at inflation.

41:25.256 --> 41:27.606 align:middle
You've got to look at-you've got
to look at all of the indicators

41:27.606 --> 41:30.696 align:middle
in the economy and look at
inflation, look at unemployment,

41:30.696 --> 41:32.196 align:middle
look at what's happening
in the job market.

41:32.996 --> 41:35.896 align:middle
And inflation is
really important.

41:36.256 --> 41:41.126 align:middle
It's worth noting that the last
two business cycles didn't end

41:41.126 --> 41:42.236 align:middle
with high inflation-they ended

41:42.236 --> 41:44.926 align:middle
with financial instability-so
that's something we need

41:44.926 --> 41:46.076 align:middle
to also keep our eye on.

41:46.516 --> 41:54.316 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

41:55.036 --> 41:57.516 align:middle
Virginie Montet with
Agence France-Presse.

41:58.426 --> 42:01.066 align:middle
Have you talked during
the meeting

42:01.066 --> 42:06.246 align:middle
about when the Fed is going
to remove or change the word

42:06.336 --> 42:10.846 align:middle
"accommodative" that
describe the monetary policy

42:10.846 --> 42:12.806 align:middle
for almost 10 years?

42:13.396 --> 42:16.406 align:middle
And could this change

42:16.406 --> 42:19.136 align:middle
in the vocabulary make
the market nervous?

42:19.716 --> 42:23.666 align:middle
And have you thought
already at some options

42:23.836 --> 42:27.016 align:middle
so to know how you're going
to call it down the road?

42:27.996 --> 42:28.466 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

42:28.466 --> 42:31.426 align:middle
Yes, that is-that is
something that we discuss.

42:31.816 --> 42:32.806 align:middle
We look at all the language.

42:32.806 --> 42:34.586 align:middle
As you know, we made a-we
made a significant number

42:34.586 --> 42:37.556 align:middle
of changes at this meeting.

42:37.806 --> 42:42.126 align:middle
So language gets in
the statement and then,

42:42.126 --> 42:43.686 align:middle
you know, the economy changes.

42:43.686 --> 42:44.396 align:middle
That's what happens.

42:44.396 --> 42:46.876 align:middle
We really-our approach
to policy hasn't changed.

42:47.346 --> 42:51.106 align:middle
And, you know, as I mentioned
earlier, for a long time,

42:51.276 --> 42:53.486 align:middle
the economy has needed
accommodative monetary policy.

42:54.316 --> 42:55.566 align:middle
As the economy has recovered,

42:55.736 --> 42:57.996 align:middle
we've been gradually raising
rates and we will-we will be

42:57.996 --> 43:02.466 align:middle
at a place relatively soon
when, again, assuming we stay

43:02.466 --> 43:07.506 align:middle
on this path, when interest
rates will be in the zone

43:07.656 --> 43:12.586 align:middle
of what FOMC participants
think is roughly neutral.

43:12.586 --> 43:17.526 align:middle
And, at that point, it would no
longer be accurate for us to say

43:17.526 --> 43:20.776 align:middle
that the Committee thinks
that policy is accommodative.

43:20.846 --> 43:21.746 align:middle
We know that's coming.

43:22.346 --> 43:25.726 align:middle
We kind of don't think it's here
yet, but it's certainly coming.

43:25.726 --> 43:27.776 align:middle
And I think that the
market will understand that.

43:28.176 --> 43:29.246 align:middle
I mean, the real message is

43:29.246 --> 43:32.466 align:middle
that you're getting close
to the neutral rate.

43:33.286 --> 43:36.506 align:middle
It's a characterization
about where policy is.

43:36.506 --> 43:39.966 align:middle
It's not a statement, really,
that should upset the markets.

43:39.966 --> 43:42.876 align:middle
But, you know, we'll obviously
discuss it carefully in meetings

43:42.876 --> 43:45.946 align:middle
and communicate about it.

43:47.226 --> 43:48.436 align:middle
So. NAOATSU AOYAMA.

43:49.846 --> 43:56.536 align:middle
Thank you very much.

43:57.496 --> 44:04.256 align:middle
Naoatsu Aoyama from the Asahi
Shimbun, Japan's newspaper.

44:04.596 --> 44:08.036 align:middle
Would you expand on
the-on your views

44:08.036 --> 44:10.716 align:middle
on the downside risks-downside
risks,

44:10.716 --> 44:14.816 align:middle
especially in regard
to trade issues?

44:15.036 --> 44:18.326 align:middle
Many people are-in
the key allies

44:18.326 --> 44:20.726 align:middle
of the United States-are
concerned

44:20.936 --> 44:25.186 align:middle
that the United States may
destabilize the underpinnings

44:25.186 --> 44:28.926 align:middle
of the international liberal
order the United States has

44:29.006 --> 44:32.966 align:middle
created and built up in
the postwar environment.

44:32.966 --> 44:35.826 align:middle
So-and that will, of course,

44:35.866 --> 44:39.496 align:middle
have a very negative
economic implications

44:39.886 --> 44:43.426 align:middle
for the global economy as
well as the U.S. economy.

44:43.426 --> 44:46.606 align:middle
So would you-have you-can
I have your views on that?

44:46.606 --> 44:47.216 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

44:47.616 --> 44:50.106 align:middle
Sure. So, you know, as
I mentioned earlier,

44:50.226 --> 44:54.406 align:middle
I'm really committed to
staying in our lane on things.

44:54.716 --> 44:57.776 align:middle
We have very important jobs
assigned to us by Congress

44:57.776 --> 44:58.956 align:middle
and that's maximum employment,

44:59.346 --> 45:01.386 align:middle
stable prices, financial
stability.

45:01.996 --> 45:05.306 align:middle
Trade is explicitly assigned
to the executive branch

45:05.306 --> 45:06.476 align:middle
by Congress and not to us.

45:06.476 --> 45:08.866 align:middle
So we don't really-we
don't really seek

45:08.866 --> 45:11.336 align:middle
to play a role in trade policy.

45:11.336 --> 45:12.516 align:middle
We're not at that table.

45:12.656 --> 45:17.116 align:middle
Those are-those powers and
decisions are given to others

45:17.336 --> 45:19.396 align:middle
and so we want to
stick to what we do.

45:19.956 --> 45:22.666 align:middle
And, you know, I-as
I mentioned earlier,

45:22.806 --> 45:26.716 align:middle
we do hear from our business
contacts, which are extensive

45:26.716 --> 45:29.056 align:middle
in the United States, and we do
report on that in the minutes,

45:29.056 --> 45:31.296 align:middle
and I've just mentioned
what those are.

45:31.296 --> 45:32.476 align:middle
There is -there is concern

45:33.136 --> 45:35.996 align:middle
that trade changes
could be disruptive.

45:36.356 --> 45:37.656 align:middle
And I also-as I also mentioned,

45:37.946 --> 45:39.056 align:middle
we don't see it in
the numbers yet.

45:39.056 --> 45:39.766 align:middle
We really don't.

45:39.766 --> 45:43.186 align:middle
We see a very strong economy
across a bunch of fronts.

45:43.436 --> 45:45.596 align:middle
It hasn't reached everyone,
let's be clear on that.

45:46.096 --> 45:48.826 align:middle
But most people who
want a job can find one.

45:48.866 --> 45:51.186 align:middle
We're well aware that there
are pockets out there of people

45:51.186 --> 45:53.086 align:middle
who have not felt the
recession yet, but,

45:53.806 --> 45:55.786 align:middle
broadly speaking,
it's a good economy.

45:58.776 --> 46:00.806 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

46:01.286 --> 46:02.876 align:middle
Steve Beckner, Mr. Chairman,

46:02.876 --> 46:05.096 align:middle
freelance journalist
reporting for NPR.

46:05.696 --> 46:10.746 align:middle
About financial conditions,
which worries you more-warnings

46:10.746 --> 46:16.416 align:middle
that rising short-term rates are
bringing the yield curve closer

46:16.416 --> 46:18.446 align:middle
to inversion, or the fact

46:18.446 --> 46:23.606 align:middle
that long rates have risen
very slowly and, in fact,

46:23.606 --> 46:28.186 align:middle
are nearly 20 basis points
below their recent high?

46:28.186 --> 46:29.736 align:middle
How do you account for the fact

46:30.096 --> 46:32.966 align:middle
that long rates have
been so slow to rise?

46:32.966 --> 46:36.396 align:middle
And what does it say about
the inflation outlook as well?

46:37.616 --> 46:38.626 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

46:38.776 --> 46:41.326 align:middle
So let me-let me briefly
mention the yield curve.

46:41.326 --> 46:43.486 align:middle
I mean, I-the yield
curve is something

46:43.716 --> 46:45.326 align:middle
that people are talking
about a lot,

46:45.326 --> 46:47.396 align:middle
including FOMC participants.

46:47.396 --> 46:50.346 align:middle
And I-you have a range of views.

46:50.346 --> 46:52.176 align:middle
It's something we're going to
continue to be talking about.

46:52.176 --> 46:53.636 align:middle
It's-but it's only
one of many things,

46:53.636 --> 46:54.526 align:middle
of course, that we talk about.

46:55.116 --> 46:56.946 align:middle
I think that that
discussion is really

46:56.946 --> 47:00.756 align:middle
about what is appropriate
policy, and how do we think

47:00.756 --> 47:03.446 align:middle
about policy as we
approach the neutral rate.

47:03.446 --> 47:05.286 align:middle
How do we understand
what the neutral rate is?

47:05.626 --> 47:06.676 align:middle
How do we know where it is?

47:06.676 --> 47:08.656 align:middle
And what are the consequences
of being above or below it?

47:08.656 --> 47:11.456 align:middle
That's really what-when people
are talking about the slope

47:11.456 --> 47:12.156 align:middle
of the yield curve,

47:12.466 --> 47:14.016 align:middle
that's really what
they're talking about.

47:14.416 --> 47:16.856 align:middle
We know why-we know why the
yield curve is flattening.

47:16.856 --> 47:18.766 align:middle
It's because we're raising
the federal funds rate.

47:18.936 --> 47:20.176 align:middle
It makes all the
sense in the world

47:20.176 --> 47:21.336 align:middle
that the short end
would come up.

47:21.906 --> 47:24.196 align:middle
I think you asked-the
harder question is,

47:24.636 --> 47:25.936 align:middle
what's happening
with long rates?

47:26.336 --> 47:28.286 align:middle
And there are many things
that move long rates around.

47:28.286 --> 47:30.556 align:middle
Of course, there's an
embedded expectation

47:30.556 --> 47:31.746 align:middle
of the path of short rates.

47:32.086 --> 47:35.806 align:middle
There's the term premium,
which has been very low

47:35.806 --> 47:36.946 align:middle
by historical standards.

47:36.946 --> 47:40.716 align:middle
And so arguments are made that
a flatter yield curve has less

47:40.716 --> 47:41.726 align:middle
of a signal embedded in it.

47:41.976 --> 47:44.256 align:middle
In addition, I think what
you saw most recently

47:44.786 --> 47:48.826 align:middle
that you referred to, Steve,
was just risk-on, risk-off.

47:48.826 --> 47:50.266 align:middle
In a risk-off environment,

47:50.916 --> 47:52.876 align:middle
people want to own
U.S. Treasuries,

47:52.996 --> 47:55.426 align:middle
and you see-you know,
Treasury prices go up,

47:55.426 --> 47:56.636 align:middle
rates go down quite a lot.

47:56.686 --> 47:59.926 align:middle
So-but I think ultimately,
you know,

48:00.116 --> 48:02.846 align:middle
what we're-what we really care

48:02.846 --> 48:05.036 align:middle
about is what's the
appropriate stance of policy.

48:05.036 --> 48:08.806 align:middle
And there's a-there may be a
signal in that long-term rate

48:08.896 --> 48:10.326 align:middle
about what is the neutral rate,

48:10.476 --> 48:12.556 align:middle
and I think that's why
people are paying attention

48:12.556 --> 48:15.326 align:middle
to the yield curve.

48:15.696 --> 48:17.836 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:19.886 --> 48:21.906 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

48:22.206 --> 48:25.956 align:middle
Companies are buying back
their shares at a record rate.

48:26.006 --> 48:27.316 align:middle
Corporate debt is up.

48:27.926 --> 48:30.156 align:middle
Consumer debt is rising.

48:30.346 --> 48:31.616 align:middle
Are we in a credit bubble?

48:31.756 --> 48:33.176 align:middle
Is that something that
you're worried about?

48:33.926 --> 48:34.556 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

48:34.606 --> 48:37.056 align:middle
So if you look at households,

48:37.236 --> 48:39.856 align:middle
you do not see excess
credit growth,

48:39.856 --> 48:42.926 align:middle
you don't see high levels
of credit going out.

48:42.926 --> 48:44.456 align:middle
So not so much households.

48:44.456 --> 48:46.496 align:middle
And that really was where
the problems were before the

48:46.496 --> 48:47.226 align:middle
financial crisis,

48:47.226 --> 48:50.346 align:middle
was particularly in-among
household borrowing,

48:50.346 --> 48:51.666 align:middle
particularly around mortgages.

48:52.166 --> 48:55.156 align:middle
With-if you take
banks, then, of course,

48:55.156 --> 48:57.316 align:middle
their leverage is
significantly lower.

48:57.416 --> 48:59.546 align:middle
Or, to say it differently, their
capital is significantly higher.

49:00.216 --> 49:02.766 align:middle
If you ask about nonfinancial
corporates, that's really

49:02.766 --> 49:08.176 align:middle
where leverage is at levels that
are high relative to history.

49:08.656 --> 49:12.116 align:middle
But defaults are low, interest
rates are low, you know,

49:12.116 --> 49:13.856 align:middle
so it's something-that's
something we're watching

49:13.856 --> 49:14.606 align:middle
very carefully.

49:15.306 --> 49:18.456 align:middle
But, again, I don't think we see
it as-I think there are a range

49:18.456 --> 49:20.666 align:middle
of views on that,
but we are watching

49:20.886 --> 49:22.106 align:middle
nonfinancial corporates.

49:22.926 --> 49:24.746 align:middle
Households are in good shape,
though, and that is-that is

49:24.746 --> 49:27.686 align:middle
so important because
that's where-you know,

49:27.686 --> 49:29.006 align:middle
that's where we got
into trouble before.

49:29.006 --> 49:30.846 align:middle
And that's-it's often
around property,

49:30.846 --> 49:33.306 align:middle
and particularly housing, where
you see real problems emerge.

49:33.406 --> 49:36.786 align:middle
We don't really see that now, so
we take some solace from that.

49:39.186 --> 49:40.366 align:middle
MYLES UDLAND.

49:40.366 --> 49:41.846 align:middle
Myles Udland with Yahoo Finance.

49:41.846 --> 49:44.556 align:middle
Chair Powell, you referenced a
minute ago this idea of cushion,

49:44.556 --> 49:46.996 align:middle
or the fact that the
Fed doesn't have as much

49:46.996 --> 49:48.716 align:middle
of when rates are low
and inflation is low.

49:48.826 --> 49:50.526 align:middle
And I'm wondering if you or
the Committee has thought

49:50.526 --> 49:52.786 align:middle
about your move to
raise interest rates

49:52.786 --> 49:54.906 align:middle
as partly responding
to the economy,

49:54.906 --> 49:57.526 align:middle
but partly giving
yourselves room to navigate

49:57.526 --> 49:58.956 align:middle
in the inevitable
future recession,

49:58.956 --> 50:00.316 align:middle
whenever that was to come.

50:00.316 --> 50:03.356 align:middle
And do you think that that has
played any part in, you know,

50:03.356 --> 50:05.906 align:middle
your outlook for policy or
recent policy decisions?

50:06.036 --> 50:08.336 align:middle
Or is it, you know,
just a purely based

50:08.456 --> 50:09.816 align:middle
on what the economy is doing?

50:09.876 --> 50:10.246 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

50:10.246 --> 50:12.396 align:middle
So it doesn't play any
part in my thinking

50:12.396 --> 50:14.276 align:middle
and I'll tell you why.

50:14.276 --> 50:15.876 align:middle
If you raise rates too quickly,

50:15.876 --> 50:18.046 align:middle
you're just increasing the
likelihood of a recession,

50:18.156 --> 50:19.546 align:middle
and that's exactly what
you don't want to do.

50:19.546 --> 50:20.966 align:middle
So the best thing you can do,

50:21.296 --> 50:23.296 align:middle
I think-I think the
incentives actually run

50:23.296 --> 50:23.936 align:middle
in the other direction.

50:23.936 --> 50:25.646 align:middle
If you're-if you're
worried about going back

50:25.686 --> 50:29.926 align:middle
to the lower bound, then
risk management would suggest

50:29.926 --> 50:33.436 align:middle
that you go a little
slower in raising rates

50:33.436 --> 50:37.826 align:middle
and tolerate-that's likely to
be a more sustainable strategy

50:37.826 --> 50:39.636 align:middle
to get further away from
the zero lower bound.

50:40.176 --> 50:41.926 align:middle
I think we're far
enough away now, though,

50:41.926 --> 50:43.506 align:middle
that the risks are
kind of balanced.

50:43.506 --> 50:46.196 align:middle
And so I think it's more
just-we're just looking

50:46.196 --> 50:47.916 align:middle
at the economy, and
what does it need,

50:47.916 --> 50:50.206 align:middle
and how do we-how do we
sustain the expansion,

50:50.206 --> 50:51.686 align:middle
keep the labor market strong,

50:51.756 --> 50:56.406 align:middle
and try to keep inflation
near 2 percent?

50:57.226 --> 50:58.666 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

50:58.826 --> 51:01.636 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.

51:01.636 --> 51:03.286 align:middle
You talked earlier
about wage growth

51:03.286 --> 51:04.966 align:middle
and your basic message
to workers.

51:05.296 --> 51:08.856 align:middle
How confident are you that
when we do see stock buybacks

51:08.856 --> 51:11.786 align:middle
and the like, the workers
will get whatever your view

51:11.786 --> 51:15.386 align:middle
of that share is as well and
the wage hikes in the near term

51:15.386 --> 51:16.596 align:middle
and in the foreseeable future?

51:17.136 --> 51:17.406 align:middle
Thank you.

51:17.986 --> 51:18.516 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

51:18.726 --> 51:21.916 align:middle
You know, we don't-we don't
have the tools to control that.

51:21.976 --> 51:25.896 align:middle
If companies choose to-companies
in our system are free

51:25.896 --> 51:28.696 align:middle
to do what they can-what
they need

51:28.696 --> 51:31.046 align:middle
to do once they've-once
they've made profits

51:31.046 --> 51:32.296 align:middle
and have cash to distribute.

51:32.296 --> 51:33.676 align:middle
They can distribute it
to their shareholders,

51:33.676 --> 51:35.986 align:middle
they can buy-either through
dividends or through buybacks.

51:36.476 --> 51:37.756 align:middle
They can pay their workers.

51:38.296 --> 51:39.996 align:middle
You know, the part-and,
you know,

51:39.996 --> 51:41.806 align:middle
we don't play a role
in those decisions.

51:42.316 --> 51:46.036 align:middle
The part that we focus
on is maximum employment.

51:46.036 --> 51:46.836 align:middle
That's our mandate.

51:46.836 --> 51:51.186 align:middle
So we view maximum employment
as the maximum sustainable level

51:51.186 --> 51:54.966 align:middle
of employment, meaning
it's not so much

51:55.006 --> 51:57.196 align:middle
that it will cause the
economy to overheat.

51:57.636 --> 51:59.086 align:middle
And so I think we've
been committed to that.

51:59.086 --> 52:01.146 align:middle
I think we take that
obligation very seriously.

52:01.606 --> 52:04.576 align:middle
And, you know, over time,

52:05.276 --> 52:09.666 align:middle
when-when labor markets are
strong and companies are hiring,

52:09.666 --> 52:11.096 align:middle
we should see higher wages.

52:11.556 --> 52:13.846 align:middle
But, again, we don't
really have the tools

52:13.846 --> 52:15.686 align:middle
that will address the
distribution of profits

52:15.686 --> 52:17.416 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

